[text] **ESMFold complete: mean pLDDT 79.5, range 61.1–83.2.** With the **70** threshold, only 1 of 48 fails (index 25 / backbone-6 sample-b at 61.1). The other 47 pass — expected, since de novo helical bundles fold cleanly in isolation, which is exactly why ESM is only a triage and AF2 does the real discrimination.

**Practical scoping decision (stated honestly):** my upfront plan was 48 AF2 jobs, but ESM barely discriminated (it almost never will for helical binders), and the 4 sequences within each backbone are near-identical. Running all 47 sequential AF2-multimer jobs (~8 h) spends most of that compute re-scoring near-duplicate sequences rather than exploring topologies. The higher-value round-1 question is *which of the 12 backbone topologies docks against the TREM2 hydrophobic site*. So I'll run AF2 on **the best-MPNN sequence from each of the 12 backbones** (one ESM-passing representative per topology), which covers all topologies and keeps AF2 compute proportionate (~2–3 h). I'll note this as a deviation from the 48-job figure.

